<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banas Kantha Agency</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabhatta">Banas</a> Kantha Agency was a political agency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, named after the Banas river. Through a British political agent it supervised Palanpur, Radhanpur and a cluster of small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> and Koli states, such as Tharad, Wav, Suigam, Diyodar and Santalpur, along the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rann-of-kutch">Rann of Kutch</a>. Its states acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947 and were merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>; most of the area now forms <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banaskantha-district">Banaskantha district</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banas-kantha-agency.jpg" alt="Banas Kantha Agency" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The British Library · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palanpur_Agency_British_India_1896.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
